---
globs: test_*.py
alwaysApply: false
---

# Pytest Testing Standards

This ruleset enforces consistent testing practices across the codebase, ensuring all tests are properly marked, documented, and structured.

## Test Markers & Organization

### Require pytest markers on all test functions

All test functions must be categorized with appropriate pytest markers (e.g., `@pytest.mark.unit`, `@pytest.mark.integration`). This allows for selective test execution and better test organization.

rule("All test functions must be marked with pytest.mark (unit, integration, etc...)") {
    select: function_declaration
    where: |
        node.name.text.match(/^test_/) &&
        !node.decorators.some(d => d.expression.text.startsWith("pytest.mark"))
    message: "All test functions must be marked with pytest.mark (unit, integration, etc...)"
    fix: |
        insertBefore(node, "@pytest.mark.unit\n")
}

### Mark async tests appropriately

Async test functions must include the `@pytest.mark.asyncio` decorator to ensure pytest-asyncio can properly execute them.

rule("Use pytest.mark.asyncio for async test functions") {
    select: function_declaration
    where: |
        node.isAsync &&
        !node.decorators.some(d => d.expression.text == "pytest.mark.asyncio")
    message: "Async test functions must be marked with pytest.mark.asyncio"
    fix: |
        insertBefore(node, "@pytest.mark.asyncio\n")
}

## Documentation

### Document test purpose with docstrings

Every test function should begin with a docstring that clearly explains what is being tested and why. This improves maintainability and helps others understand test intent.

rule("Test function must have a three-quote summary comment inside") {
    select: function_declaration
    where: |
        node.name.text.match(/^test_/) &&
        !node.body.statements[0]?.leadingComments?.some(c => c.text.trim().startsWith('"""'))
    message: "Test function must have a three-quote summary comment as the first statement"
    fix: |
        insertAt(node.body.statements[0], '    """Summary of the test."""\n')
}

## Test Structure

### Use Arrange-Act-Assert pattern

Tests should follow the AAA pattern with clear comment sections:
- `# Arrange` - Set up test data and preconditions
- `# Act` - Execute the code under test
- `# Assert` - Verify the expected outcomes

This pattern makes tests easier to read and understand.

rule("Test functions must use Arrange, Act, Assert comments") {
    select: function_declaration
    where: |
        node.name.text.match(/^test_/) &&
        !node.body.getText().includes("# Arrange") &&
        !node.body.getText().includes("# Act") &&
        !node.body.getText().includes("# Assert")
    message: "Test functions must use # Arrange, # Act, # Assert comments"
    suggestion: "Add # Arrange, # Act, # Assert comments to structure your test"
}

## Assertions

### Use Hamcrest matchers for assertions

All assertions should use Hamcrest's `assert_that()` for more readable and expressive test assertions. Hamcrest provides better error messages and more flexible matching.

Example:
```python
# Good ✅
assert_that(result, equal_to(expected))

# Avoid ❌
assert result == expected
```

rule("All assertions must use Hamcrest") {
    select: call_expression
    where: |
        node.expression.text.match(/assert/) &&
        !node.expression.text.match(/assert_that/)
    message: "Use Hamcrest's assert_that for assertions"
    suggestion: "Replace with assert_that from Hamcrest"
}

## Best Practices

### Keep conftest.py focused

`conftest.py` files should only contain pytest fixtures and configuration. Test functions belong in regular test files, not in conftest.

rule("Do not overload conftest.py, only keep meaningful fixtures") {
    select: file
    where: |
        node.path.endsWith("conftest.py") &&
        node.body.some(stmt =>
            stmt.type == "function_declaration" &&
            stmt.name.text.startsWith("test_")
        )
    message: "Do not put test functions in conftest.py; only keep meaningful fixtures"
    suggestion: "Move test functions out of conftest.py"
}

### Avoid debug statements in tests

Print statements and other debug code should be removed from tests. Tests should be clean and focus on logic and assertions.

rule("Focus on simplicity and logic in tests") {
    select: function_declaration
    where: |
        node.name.text.match(/^test_/) &&
        node.body.getText().includes("print(")
    message: "Avoid print statements in tests; focus on logic and assertions"
    suggestion: "Remove print statements from test functions"
}

### Don't test logging directly

Tests should verify business logic and behavior, not the fact that logging calls were made. Testing logging directly creates brittle tests that break when log messages change.

rule("Do not test logging directly") {
    select: function_declaration
    where: |
        node.name.text.match(/^test_/) &&
        node.body.getText().match(/assert.*logger/)
    message: "Do not test logging directly; focus on logic and outcomes"
    suggestion: "Remove assertions on logger calls"
}

## Coverage

### Test all code paths

Strive for comprehensive test coverage by creating tests for all logical branches, edge cases, and error conditions. This ensures robustness and catches regressions early.

rule("Test every path for coverage") {
    description: "Suggest creating tests for uncovered branches or paths"
    select: function_declaration
    where: |
        node.name.text.match(/^test_/) &&
        node.body.getText().match(/# TODO: test other path/)
    message: "There are uncovered paths; consider adding more tests for full coverage"
    suggestion: "Add tests for all logical branches"
}
